 scope('2011 television anime','The established Granitz community before the final Temple attack','Living members of the Granitz faction',['Other Lost Millennium factions as identical','Connected benefit recipients','The entire post-reboot world']),
 'Granitz’s community rejects a readily available network that would supply material support, choosing independence, embodied interaction and practical work instead. It is retained as an interpretive zero variant: useful human effort returns because people deliberately avoid an automatic support arrangement. Preserving jobs is not their stated primary aim, so that difference from a formal employment quota remains explicit. The community still uses advanced tools and aircraft; its policy is selective rejection of dependence rather than a total absence of technology.\n\nAuthority belongs to human community members and their leaders. A positive control estimate reflects local practical autonomy, while leaving uncertainty about legal ownership and the influence of military leadership. The sources do not establish an unconditional welfare floor within the faction, so guarantees stay unresolved. Hard work is valued and continues, but no reliable participation census is supplied. The broader Lost Millennium umbrella includes factions with different methods, and their actions are not assigned automatically to Granitz. The later teaching of independent life does not establish an identical worldwide economy.',
 {'guarantees':None,'control':0.6,'nonworking':None},
